<article class="env-proposition" id="06T5">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/06T5">Proposition <span data-tag="06T5">5.5.3.15</span>.</a> Let $F: \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}\rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{D}}$ be a functor between small $\infty $-categories and let $n$ be an integer. The following conditions are equivalent: </p>
<p><dl>
<dt>$(1)$</dt>
<dd><p>
The functor $F$ is $n$-truncated when viewed as a morphism in the $\infty $-category $\operatorname{\mathcal{QC}}$ (see Definition <a data-tag="05F9" href="/tag/05F9">4.7.2.1</a>). 
</p>
</dd>
<dt>$(2)$</dt>
<dd><p>
For every small $\infty $-category $\operatorname{\mathcal{K}}$, composition with $F$ induces an $n$-truncated morphism of Kan complexes $\operatorname{Fun}( \operatorname{\mathcal{K}}, \operatorname{\mathcal{C}})^{\simeq } \rightarrow \operatorname{Fun}( \operatorname{\mathcal{K}}, \operatorname{\mathcal{D}})^{\simeq }$. 
</p>
</dd>
<dt>$(3)$</dt>
<dd><p>
For every simplicial set $K$, composition with $F$ induces an $n$-truncated morphism of Kan complexes $\operatorname{Fun}(K, \operatorname{\mathcal{C}})^{\simeq } \rightarrow \operatorname{Fun}(K, \operatorname{\mathcal{D}})^{\simeq }$. 
</p>
</dd>
<dt>$(4)$</dt>
<dd><p>
The functor $F$ induces $n$-truncated morphisms of Kan complexes $\operatorname{Fun}(\Delta ^1, \operatorname{\mathcal{C}})^{\simeq } \rightarrow \operatorname{Fun}( \Delta ^1, \operatorname{\mathcal{D}})^{\simeq }$. 
</p>
</dd>
<dt>$(5)$</dt>
<dd><p>
The functor $F$ induces an $n$-truncated morphism of Kan complexes $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}^{\simeq } \rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{D}}^{\simeq }$. Moreover, for every pair of objects $X,Y \in \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$, the induced map of morphism spaces $\operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}(X,Y) \rightarrow \operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{D}}}( F(X), F(Y) )$ is $n$-truncated. 
</p>
</dd>
</dl></p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
<p>
<strong>Proof.</strong>
      The equivalence $(1) \Leftrightarrow (2)$ follows from Remark <a data-tag="020F" href="/tag/020F">5.5.3.5</a> and the implications $(3) \Rightarrow (2) \Rightarrow (4)$ are trivial. We next show that $(4)$ implies $(3)$. Without loss of generality, we may assume that $F$ is an isofibration. By virtue of Corollary <a data-tag="02VM" href="/tag/02VM">4.5.3.24</a>, we may assume without loss of generality that $F$ is an isofibration of $\infty $-categories. Then, for every simplicial set $K$, composition with $F$ induces a Kan fibration $\operatorname{Fun}(K, \operatorname{\mathcal{C}})^{\simeq } \rightarrow \operatorname{Fun}(K, \operatorname{\mathcal{D}})^{\simeq }$. Using Proposition <a data-tag="0566" href="/tag/0566">3.5.9.8</a>, we can reformulate condition $(3)$ as follows: 
    <p>
<dl>
<dt>$(3')$</dt>
<dd><p>
For every diagram $q: K \rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{D}}$, the Kan complex $\{ q\}  \times _{ \operatorname{Fun}(K, \operatorname{\mathcal{D}})^{\simeq } } \operatorname{Fun}(K, \operatorname{\mathcal{C}})^{\simeq }$ is $n$-truncated. 
</p>
</dd>
</dl>
<p>
       Let us now regard the diagram $q: K \rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{D}}$ as fixed. For every simplicial set $L$ equipped with a map $L \rightarrow K$, let $T(L)$ denote the Kan complex $\{  q|_{L} \}  \times _{ \operatorname{Fun}(L, \operatorname{\mathcal{D}})^{\simeq } } \operatorname{Fun}(L, \operatorname{\mathcal{C}})^{\simeq }$. Let us say that $L$ is <em>good</em> if $T(L)$ is $n$-truncated. Assuming that condition $(4)$ is satisfied, we will show that every simplicial set $L \in (\operatorname{Set_{\Delta }})_{/K}$ is good. The proof proceeds in several steps: 
    <p>
<ul>
<li class="custom-item" data-marker="(a)"><p>
The construction $L \mapsto T(L)$ carries colimits (in the category of simplicial sets equipped with a map to $K$) to limits (in the category of simplicial sets). This follows from the characterization of isomorphisms in $\operatorname{Fun}(L, \operatorname{\mathcal{C}})$ supplied by Theorem <a data-tag="01DK" href="/tag/01DK">4.4.4.4</a>. 
</p>
</li><li class="custom-item" data-marker="(b)"><p>
The collection of good objects of $(\operatorname{Set_{\Delta }})_{/K}$ is closed under coproducts. This follows from $(a)$, since the collection of $n$-truncated Kan complexes is closed under products (Remark <a data-tag="054U" href="/tag/054U">3.5.7.6</a>). 
</p>
</li><li class="custom-item" data-marker="(c)"><p>
If $L \hookrightarrow L'$ is a monomorphism of simplicial sets equipped with a map to $K$, then the restriction map $T(L') \rightarrow T(L')$ is a Kan fibration between Kan complexes. This follows from Propositions <a data-tag="01F1" href="/tag/01F1">4.4.5.1</a> and <a data-tag="01EZ" href="/tag/01EZ">4.4.3.7</a>. 
</p>
</li><li class="custom-item" data-marker="(d)"><p>
Suppose we are given a pushout diagram 
</p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix@R =50pt@C=50pt{ L_{01} \ar [r] \ar [d] &amp;  L_0 \ar [d] \\ L_1 \ar [r] &amp;  L } \]
</div>
<p>
 in the category $(\operatorname{Set_{\Delta }})_{/K}$, where the horizontal maps are monomorphisms. It follows from $(a)$ and $(c)$ that the diagram of Kan complexes 
</p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix@R =50pt@C=50pt{ T( L_{01} ) &amp;  T(L_0) \ar [l] \\ T(L_1) \ar [u] &amp;  T(L) \ar [u] \ar [l] } \]
</div>
<p>
 is a pullback square in which the horizontal maps are Kan fibrations, and therefore a homotopy pullback square (Example <a data-tag="010S" href="/tag/010S">3.4.1.4</a>). Consequently, if the simplicial sets $L_0$, $L_1$, and $L_{01}$ are good, then $L$ is also good (Corollary <a data-tag="06QK" href="/tag/06QK">3.5.9.14</a>). 
</p>
</li><li class="custom-item" data-marker="(e)"><p>
The collection of good simplicial sets is closed under the formation of retracts (in the category $(\operatorname{Set_{\Delta }})_{/K}$). See Proposition <a data-tag="06QJ" href="/tag/06QJ">3.5.7.7</a>. 
</p>
</li><li class="custom-item" data-marker="(f)"><p>
Let $L \rightarrow K$ be a morphism of simplicial sets where $L$ has dimension $\leq m$, for some integer $m$; we claim that $L$ is good. The proof proceeds by induction on $m$. Using $(b)$, $(d)$, and Proposition <a data-tag="001B" href="/tag/001B">1.1.4.12</a>, we can reduce to the case where $L = \Delta ^ m$ is a standard $m$-simplex. If $m =1$, the desired result follows our hypothesis that the functor $F$ satisfies $(4)$. The case $m = 0$ then follows from $(e)$. For $m \geq 2$, the inclusion $\operatorname{Spine}[m] \hookrightarrow \Delta ^ m$ is inner anodyne (Example <a data-tag="00JA" href="/tag/00JA">1.5.7.7</a>) and therefore induces a trivial Kan fibration $T( \Delta ^ m ) \rightarrow T( \operatorname{Spine}[m] )$. It will therefore suffice to show that $\operatorname{Spine}[m]$ is good, which follows from our inductive hypothesis. 
</p>
</li><li class="custom-item" data-marker="(g)"><p>
Let $L$ be any simplicial set equipped with a map to $K$. Using $(a)$, we see that the Kan complex $T(L)$ can be realized as the inverse limit of a tower 
</p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \cdots \rightarrow T( \operatorname{sk}_{3}(L) ) \rightarrow T( \operatorname{sk}_{2}(L) ) \rightarrow T( \operatorname{sk}_{1}(L) ) \rightarrow T( \operatorname{sk}_0(L) ), \]
</div>
<p>
 where the transition maps are Kan fibrations by virtue of $(c)$. It follows from $(f)$ that each of the Kan complexes $T( \operatorname{sk}_{m}(L) )$ is $n$-truncated. Applying Corollary <a data-tag="06QN" href="/tag/06QN">3.5.9.26</a>, we conclude that $T(L)$ is $n$-truncated. 
</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p>
      We now complete the proof of Proposition <a data-tag="06T5" href="/tag/06T5">5.5.3.15</a> by showing that conditions $(4)$ and $(5)$ are equivalent. As above, we may assume that $F$ is an isofibration of $\infty $-categories, and therefore induces a Kan fibration $\operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}( X, Y) \rightarrow \operatorname{Hom}_{ \operatorname{\mathcal{D}}}(F(X), F(Y) )$ for every pair of objects $X,Y \in \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ (Proposition <a data-tag="01P8" href="/tag/01P8">4.6.1.22</a>). Moreover, we may also assume that the map of cores $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}^{\simeq } \rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{D}}^{\simeq }$ is $n$-truncated (since this is part of assumption $(5)$, and follows from $(4)$ by the argument given above). Under these assumptions, we can use Proposition <a data-tag="0566" href="/tag/0566">3.5.9.8</a> to reformulate conditions $(4)$ and $(5)$ as follows: 
    <p>
<dl>
<dt>$(4')$</dt>
<dd><p>
For every morphism $\overline{f}: \overline{X} \rightarrow \overline{Y}$ in the $\infty $-category $\operatorname{\mathcal{D}}$, the Kan complex $T( \overline{f} ) = \{  \overline{f} \}  \times _{ \operatorname{Fun}( \Delta ^1, \operatorname{\mathcal{D}})^{\simeq } } \operatorname{Fun}( \Delta ^1, \operatorname{\mathcal{C}})^{\simeq }$ is $n$-truncated. 
</p>
</dd>
<dt>$(5')$</dt>
<dd><p>
For every morphism $\overline{f}: \overline{X} \rightarrow \overline{Y}$ in the $\infty $-category $\operatorname{\mathcal{D}}$ and every pair of objects $X,Y \in \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ satisfying $F(X) = \overline{X}$ and $F(Y) = \overline{Y}$, the Kan complex 
</p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}(X,Y)_{ \overline{f} } = \{  \overline{f} \}  \times _{ \operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{D}}}( \overline{X}, \overline{Y} ) } \operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}(X,Y) \]
</div>
<p>
 is $n$-truncated. 
</p>
</dd>
</dl>
<p>
      Fix a morphism $\overline{f}: \overline{X} \rightarrow \overline{Y}$ in $\operatorname{\mathcal{D}}$, let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_{ \overline{X} }^{\simeq }$ denote the fiber of the Kan fibration $F^{\simeq }: \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}^{\simeq } \rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{D}}^{\simeq }$ over $\overline{X}$, and define $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_{ \overline{Y} }^{\simeq }$ similarly. Since $F^{\simeq }$ is an $n$-truncated Kan fibration, the Kan complexes $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_{ \overline{X} }^{\simeq }$ and $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_{ \overline{Y} }^{\simeq }$ are $n$-truncated (Proposition <a data-tag="0566" href="/tag/0566">3.5.9.8</a>). It follows that the Kan complex $T( \overline{f} )$ is $n$-truncated if and only if the restriction map 
    <p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  T( \overline{f} ) \xrightarrow {\theta } \{  \overline{f} \}  \times _{ \operatorname{Fun}( \operatorname{\partial \Delta }^1, \operatorname{\mathcal{D}})^{\simeq } } \operatorname{Fun}( \operatorname{\partial \Delta }^1, \operatorname{\mathcal{C}})^{\simeq } \simeq \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_{ \overline{X} }^{\simeq } \times \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_{ \overline{Y} }^{\simeq } \]
</div>
<p>
       is $n$-truncated (Proposition <a data-tag="056C" href="/tag/056C">3.5.9.13</a>). It follows from Propositions <a data-tag="01F1" href="/tag/01F1">4.4.5.1</a> and <a data-tag="01EZ" href="/tag/01EZ">4.4.3.7</a> that $\theta $ is a Kan fibration. Applying Proposition <a data-tag="0566" href="/tag/0566">3.5.9.8</a> again, we see that this condition is satisfied if and only if each fiber of $\theta $ is an $n$-truncated Kan complex. The equivalence of $(4')$ and $(5')$ now follows by allowing the morphism $\overline{f}$ to vary. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
</p>
